---
title: Lentils with lemon
description: Lesson 6.2. Salt first, then acid, and what each one does.
servings: 2
time: 40 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, tasting-and-fixing]
lesson: "6.2"
technique: seasoning with salt and acid
status: draft
---

> Technique: seasoning in two moves. A plain pot of lentils is cooked
> with no seasoning at all, then brought to life at the end: first
> with salt, then with acid. Taste after every addition.

Put @green lentils{200%g}(or Puy lentils, rinsed) in a #saucepan{} with @water{700%ml}, @bay leaf{1} and @garlic{1%clove}(peeled, whole). Bring to a boil, then simmer for ~{25%minutes}, until the lentils are tender but hold their shape.

Drain the lentils, keeping a few spoonfuls of the cooking liquid. Return the lentils to the pan with 3 tablespoons of the liquid and stir in @olive oil{2%tbsp}.

Taste a spoonful. It will be flat. Add a third of @salt{3/4%tsp}, stir, and taste again. Repeat until the lentils taste full and of themselves, not salty. You may not need all of it.

> Why last: saltiness varies between ingredients, and liquid that
> boils down concentrates whatever salt is in it. Season lightly while
> cooking, then taste and adjust just before serving.
-- source: Rebecca Hays, "Is Your Dish Too Salty? Sweet? Sour? Spicy? Here's How to Fix It.", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-07-04, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/5816-is-your-dish-too-salty-sweet-sour-spicy-here's-how-to-fix-it ; "7 Tips for Successful Soup Making", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-10-10, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/6226-7-tips-for-successful-soup-making

Now add @lemon juice{1%tbsp} one teaspoon at a time, tasting after each. Stop when the lentils taste bright and before they taste sour.

> Why both: they do different jobs. In the words of a Cook's
> Illustrated editor, salt intensifies the flavour that is already
> there, while acid balances it; acid also makes the mouth water. A
> dish that tastes dull after salting usually needs acid, not more
> salt.
-- source: Eden Faithfull, "Put Down the Salt and Season Your Food with Vinegar", America's Test Kitchen, 2021-11-30, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/4073-put-down-the-salt-and-season-your-food-with-vinegar (Paul Adams, quoted; the article describes no test)

> Science: tastes interfere with each other. In a mixture, each taste
> is perceived as weaker than it would be alone, which is called
> mixture suppression, and the effect is not symmetric: saltiness
> suppresses bitterness, but bitterness does not suppress saltiness.
> Adjusting a dish means using one taste to turn another down. Look
> up: mixture suppression, taste interactions.
-- source: Rebecca Hays, "Is Your Dish Too Salty? Sweet? Sour? Spicy? Here's How to Fix It.", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-07-04, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/5816-is-your-dish-too-salty-sweet-sour-spicy-here's-how-to-fix-it ; Keast, Breslin and Beauchamp, "Suppression of Bitterness Using Sodium Salts", Chimia 55(5):441, 2001, https://doi.org/10.2533/chimia.2001.441 (abstract)

> If it goes wrong: too sour, stir in more olive oil, or a pinch of
> sugar. Too salty, a little more lemon can mask a mild case; for a
> bad one, cook more unsalted lentils and combine them.
-- source: Rebecca Hays, "Is Your Dish Too Salty? Sweet? Sour? Spicy? Here's How to Fix It.", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-07-04, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/5816-is-your-dish-too-salty-sweet-sour-spicy-here's-how-to-fix-it (fat or a sweetener for sour; an acid for salty)

Stir in @parsley{2%tbsp}(finely chopped) and serve warm.

> Next time, without the recipe: cook it plain. Salt until it tastes
> full, then acid until it tastes bright, tasting between every
> addition.
